{"answerArea":{"calculator":false,"chi2Table":false,"periodicTable":false,"tTable":false,"zTable":false},"hints":[{"content":"Notice that we can rewrite the integral as $\\displaystyle\\int(x^3+1)^6 \\cdot 3x^2dx\\,$\n\n ","images":{},"replace":false,"widgets":{}},{"content":"If we let $\\blueD {u=x^3+1}$, then $\\purpleD{du=3x^2 \\, dx}$.  \n\nSubstituting gives us:\n\n$\\displaystyle\\int(\\blueD{x^3+1})^6 \\cdot \\purpleD{3x^2dx}\\,=\\displaystyle \\int \\blueD {u}^6\\, \\purpleD{du}\\,$","images":{},"replace":false,"widgets":{}},{"content":"We recognize this antiderivative.\n\n$\\begin{align}\\phantom{\\displaystyle\\int\\dfrac{e^x}{1+e^{2x}}dx}\u0026= \\int u^6\\,du~\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\u0026=\\dfrac{u^7}{7}+C\\end{align}$\n\n","images":{},"replace":false,"widgets":{}},{"content":"We can now substitute back to find the antiderivative in terms of $x$.\n\n$\\begin{align}\\phantom{\\displaystyle\\int\\dfrac{e^x}{1+e^{2x}}dx~}\u0026=\\dfrac{u^7}{7}+C\\\\\\\\\\\\\u0026=\\dfrac{(x^3+1)^7}{7}+C\\end{align}$","images":{},"replace":false,"widgets":{}},{"content":"The answer:\n\n$\\displaystyle\\int3x^2(x^3+1)^6dx\\,=\\dfrac{(x^3+1)^7}{7}+C$","images":{},"replace":false,"widgets":{}}],"itemDataVersion":{"major":0,"minor":1},"question":{"content":" $\\displaystyle\\int3x^2(x^3+1)^6dx\\,=$\n\n[[☃ radio 1]]","images":{},"widgets":{"radio 1":{"alignment":"default","graded":true,"options":{"choices":[{"content":"$\\displaystyle\\dfrac{x^7}{7}+C$","correct":false},{"content":"$\\displaystyle\\dfrac{(x^3+1)^7}{7}+C$","correct":true},{"content":"$\\displaystyle\\dfrac{x^3(x^3+1)^7}{7}+C$","correct":false},{"content":"$\\displaystyle\\dfrac{x^3\\left(\\dfrac14x^4+x\\right)^7}{7}+C$","correct":false}],"deselectEnabled":false,"displayCount":null,"hasNoneOfTheAbove":false,"multipleSelect":false,"onePerLine":true,"randomize":true},"static":false,"type":"radio","version":{"major":1,"minor":0}}}}}
